course: dictionary
lesson: 2604
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the subject you are studying often tells you which one is meant. The word deposition is a good example. Read the words around it and ask which subject the sentence belongs to.
explain: «When water or wind slows down, it drops what it carries, a process called deposition.» Here the clues are water, wind and carries. Deposition is the dropping of sand, mud or stones, which builds up new land such as beaches and deltas.
explain: In a law lesson, the clues are different. A deposition is a statement made under oath and written down for a court. In a history lesson, a deposition can mean a leader being removed from power. Always check the clues, then try your meaning in the sentence.
fact: Deposition means dropping or putting something down, often in a new place.
fact: In geography, deposition builds new land, such as beaches, spits, deltas and floodplains.
fact: In science, deposition can be a gas turning straight into a solid, like frost forming on grass.
fact: In law, a deposition is a sworn statement written down to be used in court.
fact: In history, the deposition of a leader means removing them from power.
exercise: «When water or wind slows down, it drops what it carries, a process called deposition.» In this sentence, what does deposition mean? | Picking up sand and stones from the river bed | Freezing water into ice | *Dropping the sand, mud or stones that were being carried | Blowing the whole beach away
exercise: In a law lesson, a deposition is described as a statement made under oath, written down for a court. What is a deposition here? | A kind of rock found in caves | *A sworn statement written down for use in court | A speech given at a party | A holiday in the desert
exercise: In history, the people voted and the parliament removed the king from power. Which meaning of deposition fits this event? | A written statement for a court case | Frost forming on a window | *Removing a leader from power | Water dropping mud at a river mouth
test: On cold mornings, water vapour in the air can turn straight into white frost on the grass. Which science meaning of deposition is this? | Sand being washed out to sea | A solid melting into a liquid | *A gas turning straight into a solid | A king being crowned
test: Over many years, a river dropped mud at its mouth and slowly built a new delta. Which geography meaning fits this? | A sworn statement for a court | Removing a leader from power | *Deposition, building new land | A gas becoming a liquid
exam: Over many years the river's ___ at its mouth built a new delta, while the court studied a written ___ made under oath. | sublimation, sublimation | *deposition, deposition | deposition, erosion | erosion, deposition
